    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Lineage tracing techniques, which track cells and their progeny through development, are an essential tool to identify stem cells and characterize their regenerative capacities. The past decade has seen the emergence of lineage tracing tools that use 3-4 fluorescent proteins as labels. Such tools have been widely employed to characterize the regenerative capacity of mouse organs, including the brain, kidney, and liver. However, the small number of labels they employ limit their use in settings that exhibit cell migration, like early embryonic development, where the movement of cells makes it difficult to resolve whether certain cells share the same lineage. For the lineage tracing of these systems, a tool with a higher resolution is required.\r\nThis study demonstrates that multiple fluorescent labels can be used to mark a single cell by localizing fluorescent proteins to different cellular compartments. We then used these localized proteins to design a high-resolution reporter system that achieves 25 discernible labels by confocal microscopy. We derived and plan to validate a mouse line and mouse embryonic stem cell line that stably express the construct which we hope will be widely used for lineage tracing studies in the future.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
